+# ECON-02B2d10 — Depleted-source recovery and construction-demand planning
+
+**Date:** November 1, 2026
+**Project:** Single Galaxy original colony game
+**Exact predecessor:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d9_Priority_Autonomy_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`
+**Development:** Local Linux/C++/SQLite; GitHub remains repository storage, not a development hub.
+
+## Objective and architecture
+
+Continue ECON-02B2d9 by detecting when a reserved warehouse or a new stock source cannot serve an NPC construction site, making a physically legitimate recovery decision, and presenting bounded, priority-aware demand forecasts to the colony player. Continue to enforce one authoritative simulation writer and never mint, teleport, or double-allocate resources.
+
+The construction scheduler runs on the existing colony simulation thread. `Simulation` owns all material custody (ship, site depot, installed storage nodes, reserved stock, workers' cargo, construction deliveries and completed physical structures), while `NpcConstructionCrews` owns durable assignments and scheduling. Changing actual stock still requires the existing journaled `Action::ReserveConstructionModules`, pickup, delivery, return and build commands; forecast data is read-only.
+
+## Changes
+
+1. **Route-proof distinction.** A route check returns `Yes`, `No`, or `Unknown` rather than conflating a failed bounded search with a blocked route. Straight two-leg Manhattan paths are checked first; a bounded breadth-first search then attempts local detours. `No` is returned only when a connected component is exhausted or a coordinate is invalid. When the 4,096-node CPU budget is exhausted, the result is `Unknown` and inventory is not moved on the basis of an unproven route. The CPU budget is not a factory size limit.
+2. **Stranded reservation recovery.** Before an NPC travels to a reserved node, the scheduler checks the worker-to-source and source-to-build-site routes. On a proven disconnection, it releases only the uncollected reservation back to the **same physical source** via a journaled, authoritative reserve/release action. Already carried modules, installed structures and delivered stock are never reassigned. A reachable alternative source can then be selected on a later simulation tick.
+3. **Uninstalled source handling.** A missing/de-installed reserved node is reported as `RESERVED_SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE` and its physical custody is left in the world ledger. It is **not** magically transferred to the depot: a physical salvage/removal workflow is a future dependency. API reports stranded unreserved and stranded reserved quantities separately.
+4. **Reachability before allocating.** The scheduler only reserves reachable, installed node stock whose source can reach the actual current construction cell. It also validates the depot path before staging ship modules, avoiding fruitless resupply when no route can serve the site. It reports distinct reasons for `SOURCE_ROUTE_BLOCKED`, `SOURCE_ROUTE_UNVERIFIED`, `SUPPLY_ROUTE_BLOCKED`, `SUPPLY_ROUTE_UNVERIFIED`, `DEPOT_ROUTE_BLOCKED`, and `SUPPLY_SHORTAGE`.
+5. **Deterministic read-only demand forecast.** Every visible plan exposes its remaining embodied module demand and module quantity not already committed via a reservation, named NPC cargo or the current construction-cell delivery. Free depot and installed-node inventory is projected against active plans in descending priority, then ascending plan ID order. Each individual free module is counted at most once in this forecast; routes must be proven reachable to count it. `projectedLocalFunding` and `projectedLocalGap` are expressly estimates, not reservations, procurement authorizations, or promises that staff will be assigned. The forecast does not count physically stranded nodes as available sources.
+6. **SQLite read-cursor lifecycle repair.** Closed the active `sg_structure_plans` read cursor before performing the authoritative world resource action. A real regression test exposed `database is locked` from attempting to update a crew job after a world write while the crew connection retained its previous read snapshot. The repaired sequencing persists the expected reason and allows restarts.
+7. **Colony UI and protocol.** Factory/crew listing adds demand, projected local funding/gap, stranded stock, and stranded reservations. The colony construction panel shows them alongside existing warehouse and crew positions. No new public write capability, no competing world writer, and no separate UI/application were introduced.
+
+## Tests performed
+
+| Test | Outcome |
+| --- | --- |
+| Dedicated `ECON02B2d10_SourceRecovery` native test | PASS |
+| Existing `ECON02B2d9_PriorityAllocator` | PASS |
+| Existing `ECON02B2d8_MultiSourceAllocations` | PASS |
+| Existing authenticated `ECON02B2d6_NPCConstruction_HTTP` | PASS (real colony process, 10.35 s) |
+| JavaScript `node --test tests/*.js` | 93/93 PASS |
+| Offline Chromium crew, stock and control test | PASS |
+| Offline Chromium colony structure editing test | PASS |
+| Affected Linux native kernel, source tests, and colony HTTP host | Compiled successfully |
+| Entire native project / all CTest targets | NOT VERIFIED in this milestone: the attempted full build was interrupted by the execution limit during older targets |
+| Native Windows executable / actual Windows GUI | NOT VERIFIED |
+
+The new native test exercises an actual built storage node surrounded on all four sides by physical walls, after a reservation was already made. It proves the reservation moves back into the same enclosed warehouse, that the alternate storage node is then chosen and used to complete the structure, that no modules are teleported or duplicated, and that SQLite restart preserves the finished fixture and the trapped physical inventory. A separate newly enclosed work cell confirms that no amount of available ship stock can silently cross a wall and that an actionable `SUPPLY_ROUTE_BLOCKED` reason is emitted. It also tests a two-project, single-stock budget: the higher priority plan gets the only two modules in the non-mutating forecast, while the lower plan has a two-module projected local gap. The forecast is proven to leave the world snapshot unchanged.
+
+## Limitations / follow-on gates
+
+- `Route::Unknown` is an explicit limitation of bounded CPU effort and does **not** establish that the path is blocked. Long or maze-heavy routes need future hierarchical 3D navigation and an incremental route validation worker. No arbitrary floor-size or factory-tier area caps were added.
+- Inaccessible material remains at its source; abandoned/destroyed warehouse salvage and actual inventory removal are not implemented here. Construction projects may need player intervention to open doors, demolish barriers or clear a route.
+- No new automatic ore mining, goods procurement, NPC vehicle transport, or inter-colony material shipping was added. Demand forecasts currently cover local construction modules and current priority/active policy, not a galaxy-wide industrial material requirements planning (MRP) solver.
+- Demand projections are site-local and non-binding. They exclude NPC position congestion, fatigue, future production and inventory promised to unrelated subsystems. They cannot replace authoritative reservations.
+- Full rebuild and full native regression remain a follow-on verification gate. Windows GUI acceptance and 3D NPC movement remain open.
+
+## Changed source surfaces
+
+- `src/NpcConstructionCrews.cpp` — route certification, safe release and source selection, reservation/restart cursor fix, demand projections
+- `include/galaxy/NpcConstructionCrews.hpp` — typed projected demand fields
+- `src/factory_colony_protocol.hpp` — demand and stranded-stock JSON
+- `viewer/structures.js` — colony construction supply/demand diagnostics
+- `tests/ECON02B2d10SourceRecoveryTests.cpp` — physical route, conservation, restart and forecast test
+- `CMakeLists.txt` — native CTest registration
+- This engineering report
+
+**Next:** Warehouse decommissioning, physical salvage of stranded material, and supply-demand fulfillment with NPC-authorized restocking/production orders, retaining the same simulation authority and per-site inventory ledger.
